This essay is really worth reading. It is extraordinary to follow the contortions the author goes through to appease their audience, to caveat any admission of the reality of how well these tools work, and to desperately find something that can justify their prejudice against LLMs. It’s ethnographically an extraordinary text, and I hope there are folks studying AI denialism right now—it would make a great PhD thesis.

Four years ago, I argued that AI ethics work should be grounded in a deep understanding of the technology being assessed. Now, the folks who think themselves most focused on current harms are in an epistemic blind alley, mutually enforcing their position with thick moral criticism. For the denialists’ own sake, I can’t wait for this to change! Using transformative technology should not induce guilt. Build your side projects and contribute to a positive technological future.

