When I was a baby, my dad was driving me late at night when a drunk driver asleep at the wheel hit us head-on. My dad was in a coma for weeks, and I needed hundreds of stitches, leaving me with a massive scar. Many years later, I worked at Waymo during the early years of testing, launching, and scaling in San Francisco. There was a huge amount to do, but it felt amazing to see logs showcasing superhuman moments.

It's easy to dismiss self-driving cars as just another expensive Uber competitor for techies who don't want to talk to people. However, humans behind the wheel are one of the top causes of death and injury globally. One day, self-driving cars will make the kinds of accidents that happened to me a thing of the past.

